The code statements that lead to the bug are:
devm_request_threaded_irq(dev, client->irq, NULL, bq256xx_irq_handler_thread, IRQF_TRIGGER_FALLING | IRQF_ONESHOT, dev_name(&client->dev), bq);
bq256xx_power_supply_init(bq, &psy_cfg, dev);
Reason:
During probe, the IRQ is requested before the power supply is registered. Because devm resources are freed in reverse order during removal, the power supply gets freed before the IRQ handler is unregistered. An interrupt can then fire after the power supply is freed but before the IRQ handler is removed, causing a use-after-free when `power_supply_changed()` accesses the freed handle. Additionally, during probe, an interrupt could arrive before `bq256xx_power_supply_init` registers the power supply, leading to an uninitialized handle being used.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
ret = bq256xx_power_supply_init(bq, &psy_cfg, dev);
devm_request_threaded_irq(dev, client->irq, NULL, bq256xx_irq_handler_thread, IRQF_TRIGGER_FALLING | IRQF_ONESHOT, dev_name(&client->dev), bq);
Reason:
By moving `bq256xx_power_supply_init` before `devm_request_threaded_irq`, the power supply is registered first. During removal, devm releases resources in reverse order: the IRQ handler is unregistered before the power supply is freed, eliminating the race window. During probe, the power supply is available before the IRQ handler is installed, so any interrupt occurring cannot use an uninitialized or freed handle. This fixes both the use-after-free and the uninitialized-handle scenarios.

